    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lung cancer is the deadliest type of cancer and a cure for advanced stages of this disease does not currently exist. MYC and KRAS are two oncogenes commonly implicated in the development and maintenance of lung cancer. Emerging evidence has revealed that lung cancer reprograms cellular metabolism and previous studies have demonstrated that MYC and KRAS alter glucose and glutamine metabolism as a means of supporting cancer cell survival. However, the role that MYC and KRAS play in lipid metabolism in lung cancer has not been addressed. Here, preliminary data using Desorption Electrospray Ionization Mass Spectrometry Imaging (DESI-MSI) for lipid profiling of lung tumor tissue as well as gene expression analysis of key lipogenesis genes show that MYC and KRAS not only induce lipogenesis in lung tumors but do so through distinct pathways. Additionally, by administering inhibitors of enzymes involved in the fatty acid synthesis pathway to human lung cancer cell lines, I demonstrate that blocking lipogenesis reduces the proliferation of lung tumor cells in vitro. These results suggest that lung tumors are dependent upon oncogene-induced lipogenesis and that inhibition of lipogenesis pathways may be a promising therapeutic approach for the treatment of lung cancer.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Sullivan, Delaney and Felsher, Dean and Gozani, Or. (2016). MYC and RAS induce lipogenesis in lung cancer and generate distinct tumor subtype lipid signatures. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/jd878vd0930",
